			<div id="content-wrapper">
				<div class="wsite-section-content">
					<div class="container"><div class="wsite-elements wsite-not-footer">
	<table id="blogTable" class="wsite-not-footer" style="border: 0; width: 100%; table-layout: fixed">
	<tbody><tr>
	    <td valign="top">
	        	<div id="655425528249031429-blog" class="blog-body" style="float: left;"> 
		<div id="wsite-content">	<div id="blog-post-688723408859501491" class="blog-post">
	
	
		<div class="blog-header">
			<h2 class="blog-title">
					<a class="blog-title-link blog-link" href="//www.ourcookingjourney.com/recipe-pages/pesto-al-cavolo-nero-tuscan-kale-pesto">Pesto Al Cavolo Nero - Tuscan Kale Pesto</a>

			</h2>
			<p class="blog-date">
					<span class="date-text">
		29/9/2026
	</span>

			</p>
			<p class="blog-comments">
					<a href="//www.ourcookingjourney.com/recipe-pages/pesto-al-cavolo-nero-tuscan-kale-pesto#comments" class="blog-link">
		1 Comment
	</a>

			</p>
		</div>
	
		<div class="blog-separator">&nbsp;</div>
	
		<div class="blog-content">
				<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="/uploads/1/2/4/7/124776650/published/tuscan-kale-pesto-pesto-al-cavolo-nero-francesco-mattana.png?1790680193" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%"> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph">Cavolo nero or Tuscan kale is a staple in Tuscan cuisine and you will find it in many of their regional dishes. One of my favourite way to cook with it is making a delicious, seasonal, vibrant pesto!<br>It's so good for you, it's packed with essential vitamins, minerals and fibre and it gives the pesto a deep, earthy flavour. You can also add a few anchovies in the pesto if you like for an extra layer of savouriness. You can use the pesto however you prefer, from a simple bruschetta to pasta, risotto - you name it! I served mine with trenette, a pasta shape similar to linguine.<br></div>  <div>  <!--BLOG_SUMMARY_END--></div>  <div class="paragraph"><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">Serves 4</span><br><br><strong style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">Ingredients</strong><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)"></span><br><ul><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)"></span>400g trenette,&nbsp;<span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">linguine or spaghetti</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">200g cavolo nero</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">40g toasted walnuts</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">40g blanched almonds</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">1 small garlic clove, or ½ clove for a milder flavour</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">60g Grana Padano cheese, finely grated</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">80-100ml extra virgin olive oil, plus extra if needed</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">2 anchovy fillets (optional)</span></li><li><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">Fine sea salt &amp; freshly ground black pepper</span></li></ul><span style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)"><br></span><strong style="color:rgb(0, 0, 0)">Method<br><br></strong>Bring a large saucepan of salted water to the boil and meanwhile tear the stems out of the cavolo and discard.&nbsp;<br>Add the cavolo nero to the boiling water&nbsp;and blanch for 3 minutes, until the leaves are tender but still retain their deep green colour.&nbsp;Drain well and leave to cool slightly.<br><span></span>Add the walnuts, almonds, garlic, grated Grana Padano cheese and anchovies, if using, to a food processor. Add the cavolo nero and a generous pinch of black pepper, then pulse a few times until roughly chopped.<br><span></span>Pour in the olive oil and blitz it until it combines all together.&nbsp;After adding the first 80ml of olive oil if it is still very thick, add a few tbsp of ice cold water and blitz again until you have a thick, creamy pesto.&nbsp;<br><br>Taste and season with salt but remember that the Grana Padano and anchovies will already add some saltiness, so season gradually.<br><br>Bring a large pot of salted water to a boil and cook the pasta until al dente. When the pasta is fully cooked, add it to a bowl with a drizzle of olive oil. Add the pesto to the pasta with a ladle of pasta water. Give it a good stir all together and serve.&nbsp;<br><br><strong>TOP TIP: </strong>It's best to eat the pesto&nbsp;immediately or the next day.&nbsp;Or transfer it to a clean and sterilised jar, cover the surface with a thin layer of olive oil and refrigerate it for up to 5 days.&nbsp;<br><br><span></span></div>  <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div> <a class="wsite-button wsite-button-large wsite-button-highlight" href="javascript:;"> <span class="wsite-button-inner">Button Text</span> </a> <div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div></div>  <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div> <a class="wsite-button wsite-button-large wsite-button-highlight" href="https://www.ourcookingjourney.com/my-cookbook.html" target="_blank"> <span class="wsite-button-inner">order my cookbook</span> </a> <div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div></div>  <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div> <a class="wsite-button wsite-button-large wsite-button-highlight" href="https://www.ourcookingjourney.com/recipes.html" target="_blank"> <span class="wsite-button-inner">Back to recipes</span> </a> <div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div></div>

		</div>
	
	
			<div class="blog-social  ">
